      "content": "I apologize for the earlier response; I understand you need a clear, technical explanation for your diesel engine exam without the metaphors. Here is the mechanical process of how a turbocharger increases intake pressure:\n\n1.  **Exhaust Energy Conversion:** Hot, high-pressure exhaust gases exit the engine cylinders and enter the turbine housing. As these gases expand and lose pressure, they transfer their kinetic energy to the turbine blades (the back half of the turbo). This causes the turbine shaft to spin at high speeds.\n2.  **Shaft Coupling:** The turbine and compressor are mounted on a single, continuous shaft. Therefore, the rotational speed of the turbine directly drives the compressor at the same RPM.\n3.  **Compression Process:** The compressor (the front half) consists of an impeller (rotor) and a diffuser (stator). As the impeller spins, it accelerates the incoming ambient air, creating a low-pressure area that draws air into the compressor inlet.\n4.  **Pressure Rise:** The air is forced through the impeller blades, gaining velocity and pressure. It then enters the diffuser, where the blade angle changes to slow the air down. This deceleration converts the kinetic energy",
